cartridge "KNOCKOUT/AHOY! 1986/2026"

' KNOCKOUT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Knockout" from Ahoy! January 1986
' (Commodore 64: a BASIC loader that pokes 1.5 KB of machine code at
' $C000; disk ahoy1986-01, file KNOCKOUT; the program, the disk and the
' sources checked name no author). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI
' made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/knockout-ml.dis), the game is rebuilt for the
' ColecoVision with tile graphics, smooth sprites and sound.
'
' The game: a boxing ring seen from above, two boxers, one round of two
' minutes. The boxers always turn to face each other. Walk with the stick,
' punch with the button. A punch that lands scores a point and knocks the
' other boxer back, dazed for a moment. 100 landed punches is a knockout;
' otherwise the bell at 0:00 ends the fight on points.
'
' From the original (orig/): the round of 2:00 shown as a clock (the C64
' read its time-of-day clock), one or two players (port 1 and port 2
' joysticks), the computer boxer for one player with two levels, a point
' per landed punch, 100 for a knockout (the C64 printed "KO" next to the
' winner's count), the boxers facing each other wherever they stand, a
' landed punch pushing the other boxer back for 15 frames, the boxers
' pushed apart for 6 frames when they bump, walking a pixel a frame in
' eight directions inside the ropes, the punch held down repeating, the
' blue (C64: white) boxer starting in the top-left corner and the red
' (C64: black) in the bottom-right one, the computer walking straight at
' its opponent and level 2 punching from further off than level 1.
'
' Liberties: on the C64 any sprite touching a boxer's head counted, even a
' body; here only the punching glove at full stretch counts, once per
' punch, tested against the other boxer's head. Eight facings instead of
' four diagonal ones. A stamina gauge (four pips): a punch costs one pip
' and they refill over time, so holding the button down or throwing
' punches at the air leaves a boxer slow when it matters (the C64 let a
' held button punch every 16 frames for ever: standing still with the
' button held was the best play). A dazed boxer can't punch. At the bell
' the winner on points is announced (the C64 only stopped). The computer
' also backs off when out of stamina and sidesteps now and then, and
' reacts a set time late (18 frames at level 1, 9 at level 2) so it is
' beatable. Colours: the C64's white and black boxers are blue and red
' corners with headguards. Sounds and music are new.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Stick: walk. Either side button: punch (hold
' to keep punching while stamina lasts). Blue uses controller 1, red
' controller 2; against the computer, either controller is blue. On the
' title, up and down choose a line, left, right or a button change it, a
' button on START begins.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs sack-bcd-element-carry removed 2026-11-01.

